 # Pruning and Reduction
 
+Also called: Gaussian Pruning, Gaussian Reduction, Visibility Pruning, Vertex Pruning, Attribute Pruning
 
 ## Overview
-Pruning and reduction methods remove redundant or low-impact 3D Gaussians from a trained or training model to reduce memory footprint and rendering latency while preserving visual fidelity [S2, S3]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on geometric simplification of primitives or opacity-independent truncation, this family focuses on the selection and elimination of entire Gaussian entities based on their contribution to the rendered image or their geometric degeneracy [G]. It is distinct from [Gaussian Representation and Truncation](12855-gaussian-representation-and-truncation.md) by actively modifying the set of Gaussians $\{G_i\}$ rather than just their rendering parameters [G].
+Pruning and reduction methods optimize the 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) representation by removing redundant or low-impact Gaussians to reduce memory footprint and increase rendering speed, or by simplifying the geometric primitives themselves. Unlike standard opacity-based pruning which simply removes Gaussians with low opacity, this family includes methods that address spatial quality imbalance [S1, unofficial], joint deletion effects [S1, unofficial], and storage overhead of view-dependent attributes [S1, unofficial]. It also encompasses vertex-level simplification for mixed-primitive representations [S1, unofficial] and visibility-based filtering for rendering acceleration [S3].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation [S1, unofficial][S2, S3]. For the shared rendering principles, mathematical formulation, and color/opacity variations, see [Gaussian Representation and Truncation](12855-gaussian-representation-and-truncation.md).
+All methods in this family operate on the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting representation, where the scene is defined by a set of $N$ 3D Gaussians $\{G_i\}_{i=1}^N$ [G]. Each Gaussian $G_i$ is defined by its mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}_i$, covariance matrix $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i$ (derived from rotation $\mathbf{R}_i$ and scale $\mathbf{S}_i$), opacity $\alpha_i$, and view-dependent color $\mathbf{c}_i(\omega)$ [G]. The rendering process evaluates the Gaussian density along a ray $\mathbf{r}(t) = \mathbf{o} + t\omega$ and integrates contributions [G]. The core variation across methods lies in the definition, storage, and optimization of $\mathbf{c}_i(\omega)$ and potentially $\alpha_i$, moving beyond the fixed SH coefficients used in base 3DGS [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Vertex-level Pruning in Mixed-Primitive Gaussian Splatting](13983-vertex-level-pruning-in-mixed-primitive-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Geometric simplification for mixed-primitive splats (triangles and lines) that removes redundant vertices to convert them into lower-order primitives (ellipses or lines), refining the surface representation. Choose when using mixed-primitive frameworks that require vertex-level optimization during training.
+Vertex-level pruning is a geometric simplification mechanism applied during the training of Mixed-Primitive Gaussian Splatting (MP-GS) to remove redundant vertices from Gaussian triangles and lines, converting them into lower-order primitives (ellipses or lines). It addresses the issue of "messy" representations where high-order primitives persist in regions that can be adequately modeled by simpler shapes, thereby refining the surface representation and reducing storage overhead. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting which only performs primitive-level opacity pruning, this method operates on the vertex level within mixed-primitive frameworks. It builds on [Pruning and Reduction](14909-pruning-and-reduction.md).
 
-### [SplatQ](14415-splatq.md)
-Post-training pruning framework that partitions Gaussians into blocks and uses Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) to jointly select Gaussians, addressing spatial quality imbalance and joint deletion effects. Choose when post-training compression with balanced regional quality is needed.
+### SplatQ
+SplatQ is a post-training pruning framework for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that addresses spatial quality imbalance and ignores joint deletion effects inherent in global Top-K selection methods [S1, unofficial]. It partitions Gaussians into size-limited blocks based on spatial and appearance similarity, allocates retention quotas to balance regional budgets, and uses a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) model to jointly select Gaussians within each block under a fixed global count [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### [Attribute Pruning](18516-attribute-pruning.md)
-Selectively reduces per-Gaussian attribute storage, primarily spherical harmonics coefficients, by dynamically tailoring SH degree to local scene complexity. Choose when optimizing memory usage and rendering efficiency without altering geometric primitives.
+Attribute pruning selectively reduces the storage and computational load of per-Gaussian attributes, primarily spherical harmonics (SH) coefficients, to optimize memory usage and rendering efficiency. Unlike sibling methods that modify the primitive count or quantize data types, this method dynamically adjusts the precision of view-dependent color representation per Gaussian based on local scene complexity.
 
