 ## Background
-Pruning in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a solution to the high memory consumption and computational cost associated with the large number of Gaussians required for high-fidelity reconstruction [G]. Early methods relied on simple opacity thresholds, but later approaches introduced geometric constraints (vertex pruning for mixed primitives) and optimization-based selection (SplatQ, learnable masks) to maintain quality at lower densities [G]. Visibility-based pruning leverages the fact that occluded Gaussians contribute little to the final image, allowing for aggressive reduction in complex scenes [S2].
+The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting method [G] uses a fixed set of Gaussians optimized via differentiable rendering. As the number of Gaussians grows, memory and rendering costs increase. Initial pruning methods focused on opacity thresholds [G]. Subsequent research introduced more sophisticated pruning strategies: vertex-level pruning for mixed primitives [S1, unofficial], block-based joint selection to handle spatial imbalance [S1, unofficial], attribute reduction for SH coefficients [S1, unofficial], learnable masks for dynamic reduction [S2], and visibility-based filtering for real-time rendering acceleration [S3].
 
